[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pdf-specification-sheet\",\n    \"title\": \"PDF Document Technical Specification Sheet | ToolTari\",\n    \"description\": \"A comprehensive reference sheet detailing PDF versions, objects structure, compression parameters, and metadata standards.\",\n    \"category\": \"pdf\",\n    \"introduction\": \"This technical resource sheet provides an overview of the Portable Document Format (PDF) specification standards. It is designed as a developer and power-user reference, covering file structures, catalog dictionaries, object types, compression parameters, and metadata schemas defined under the ISO 32000 specification guidelines.\",\n    \"content_sections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"The Core PDF File Structure\",\n        \"text\": \"A standard PDF file consists of four main parts: 1. Header: Specifies the PDF version (e.g., %PDF-1.7). 2. Body: Contains the objects that form the document (text, images, fonts). 3. Cross-Reference Table (xref): Maps object numbers to byte offsets, allowing random access rendering. 4. Trailer: Specifies the root directory dictionary and locate parameters for the xref table. This layout ensures that document readers can locate and render any page immediately without reading the entire document.\",\n        \"code_block\": \"%PDF-1.7\\n1 0 obj\\n<< /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R >>\\nendobj\\n2 0 obj\\n<< /Type /Pages /Kids [3 0 R] /Count 1 >>\\nendobj\\n3 0 obj\\n<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 4 0 R >>\\nendobj\\nxref\\n0 4\\n0000000000 65535 f\\n0000000009 00000 n\\n0000000058 00000 n\\n0000000115 00000 n\\ntrailer\\n<< /Size 4 /Root 1 0 R >>\\nstartxref\\n230\\n%%EOF\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Standard PDF Object Types\",\n        \"text\": \"PDF document graphs are composed of eight basic types of objects: Boolean values (true or false), Numbers (integers and real numbers), Strings (wrapped in parentheses, e.g., (Hello World)), Names (preceded by a slash, e.g., /Type), Arrays (wrapped in brackets, e.g.,